2 EVEN Record
3 ROLE 10109:46596222:
1 NOTE Nicholas married Eliza Bakewell, sister of Lucy who married John James Audubon, the naturalist. We have picked up bits and pieces of information from the many biographies about Audubon. In searching the AIS, I found Nicholas listed in New Y
2 CONC ork City, 15th Ward on the 1840 Census. We have no record of when he left Kentucky or if more of the family was there...??(*)
2 CONT  According to family records and books, he and Eliza were married at Fatland Ford, PA 6 Mar 1816. (found they were married in Hendersonville, KY) Not sure if there were additional living children or not. One older sister was left in a conve
2 CONC nt in France when they fled. Some history books and newspaper articles say he was in business in St. Louis with Audubon. Others show him as piloting the packet Eagle? in the Natches trade. The family was to have been in the boat building bu
2 CONC siness in the early 1800's.
2 CONT  (*)1999: Have copies of letters Lucy wrote to relatives in England. They tell of some of the moves of the family up to about 1845. His warehouse burned in New York City as a "breakfire" to save other buildings and the family moved to St. L
2 CONC ouis abt 1841-43. Looks like fire was to be the bane of the business. The warehouse burned there in the big fire of 1849. He died of a heart attack in Nov 49 according to the Church record. I'm anxious to get their wills and see what is wha
2 CONC t. (got both of their wills, $150., in 2000/2001. Sent them to Jeri with the rest of the Berthoud info back from AGB.
2 CONT 
2 CONT  ACTION: sift fact from fiction and evaluate from the `preponderance' of the evidence!
2 CONT 
2 CONT  CENSUS: KY 1820 Fed Census Shippingport, Jefferson Co. Roll M33-24 pg 30
2 CONT  AIS US 1820-1829 Berthoud, Nicholas in Jefferson Co, KY 030 Shippingport 1820
2 CONT  AIS US 1830-1839 "Berthend", Nicholas in Jefferson C, KY 151 Shippingport 1830
2 CONT  AIS US 1840-1849 "Berthond", Nicholas New York Co, NY 026 NYC 15th Ward 1840
2 CONT  LAND RECDs: FD SCFR Sec36/Twn16So/Range01West/3rd Meridian/9828 acres for $200 on June 30, 1831 Vol 10, pg 342, ID 229787
2 CONT 
2 CONT  Buried in Bellefontaine Cemetery in St. Louis, MO
2 CONT  Note: Col. Alexander C. Berthoud Chi Psi From Lawyers & Lawmakers of KY by H. Levin 1897
2 CONT 
2 CONT  Nicholas married Eliza Bakewell, sister of Lucy who married John James Audubon, the naturalist. We have picked up bits and pieces of information from the many biographies about Audubon. In searching the AIS, I found Nicholas listed in New 
2 CONC York City, 15th Ward on the 1840 Census. We have no record of when he left Kentucky or if more of the family was there...??(*)
2 CONT  According to family records and books, he and Eliza were married at Fatland Ford, PA 6 Mar 1816. (found they were married in Hendersonville, KY) Not sure if there were additional living children or not. One older sister was left in a conve
2 CONC nt in France when they fled. Some history books and newspaper articles say he was in business in St. Louis with Audubon. Others show him as piloting the packet Eagle? in the Natches trade. The family was to have been in the boat building bu
2 CONC siness in the early 1800's.
2 CONT  (*)1999: Have copies of letters Lucy wrote to relatives in England. They tell of some of the moves of the family up to about 1845. His warehouse burned in New York City as a "breakfire" to save other buildings and the family moved to St. L
