3 ROLE 40001:464132960:
1 NOTE Bakewell, Rev. Alexander Gordon.--Residence, 1619 South Rampart street, New
2 CONT Orleans, La--Was born at Louisville, Ky., Dec. 16, 1822; son of Thomas
2 CONT Woodhouse and Elizabeth Rankin (Page) Bakewell, both of whom were born in
2 CONT England, and came to the United States about the beginning of the nineteenth
2 CONT century.  The paternal and maternal grandparents, William Bakewell and
2 CONT Benjamin Page, established the first glassmaking plant in the United States,
2 CONT at Pittsburg, Pa.., in 1808, and this property remained in the family until
2 CONT quite recently.  An ancestor, whose name also was William Bakewell, was one of
2 CONT the founders of the science of geology.  He lived in England.  Another of the
2 CONT family ancestors occupied the office of Lord Mayor of London, and it was he
2 CONT who, upon a memorable occasion, supplied means to Charles II.  Members of the
2 CONT Bakewell family were in banking business in London during many generations. 
2 CONT In 1808 Thomas Woodhouse Bakewell, the father, moved to New Orleans and there
2 CONT established himself in mercantile business, which he continued until the
2 CONT beginning of the war of 1812, when he moved to Louisville, Ky.  While living
2 CONT in the latter city he built the first steamboat to ply between Louisville and
2 CONT New Orleans.  Later he moved to Covington, Ky., and from there to Cincinnati,
2 CONT where his death occurred.  His profession was that of a shipbuilder and
2 CONT foundryman.  Alexander Gordon Bakewell was educated at Elizabeth college,
2 CONT Island of Guernsey.  In early life he was engaged in mercantile pursuits,
2 CONT having received a commercial education in the house of Francisco de Lizardi,
2 CONT at New Orleans, with which business he became associated in 1839, upon his
2 CONT return from college.  At the beginning of the Civil war he enlisted as orderly
2 CONT sergeant and lay reader in Company Five, Washington artillery, and on March 6,
2 CONT 1862, went to the front.  He took part in the battle of Shiloh, and in all the
2 CONT engagements from Dalton down to Atlanta.  After having been honorably
2 CONT discharged from the Confederate service, he re-entered the army of the
2 CONT Confederacy as chaplain, with a commission from the Confederate States
2 CONT Government, and was assigned to duty in the Seventh and Twenty-Eight
2 CONT Mississippi battalions at Snyder's Bluff, near Vicksburg.  In this connection
2 CONT he served throughout the siege of Vicksburg, and after the surrender of the
2 CONT army there was paroled.  After exchange he served with his command as above
2 CONT stated to the close of the war and was paroled at the surrender of the army of
2 CONT the Tennessee, at Gainesville, Ala., May 12 1865.  He then returned to New
2 CONT Orleans, where he has since lived, with the exception of 15 years passed in
2 CONT the parishes of East and West Feliciana, La.  He was ordained as Deacon in the
2 CONT Protestant Episcopal church by Bishop Wilmer, of Alabama, May 13, 1864, and
2 CONT ordained as Priest by the same bishop, May 7, 1865.  Upon his return to New
2 CONT Orleans after the war, he took charge of Mt. Olivet church, Algiers, where he
2 CONT remained during several years.  Following this he was rector of the church at
2 CONT Clinton, La., during three years, and thereafter Rector of Grace church, St.
2 CONT Francisville, La., until 1884, when he took charge of Trinity chapel, New
2 CONT Orleans, where he has since remained, being at this time incumbent of that
2 CONT charge.  Rev. Bakewell is affiliated with the democratic party.  He is a
2 CONT Master Mason and chaplain of Alpha Home Lodge, Free and Accepted Masons. 
2 CONT Also, he is chaplain-general of the United Confederate veterans, Louisiana
2 CONT division.  In 1847 W. A. Gordon Bakewell was married to Miss Sophie Cuvellier,
2 CONT daughter of Charles and Virginia (Coigner) Cuvellier of New Orleans, La., and
2 CONT three children have been born to their union, namely:  Theore H., Emma, and
2 CONT Annie Bakewell, the first and last-named of whom are now deceased.
2 CONT 
2 CONT Source:  Louisiana:  Comprising Sketches of Parishes, Towns, Events,
2 CONT Institutions, and Persons, Arranged in Cyclopedic Form (volume 3), pp. 34-35. 
2 CONT Edited by Alcée Fortier, Lit.D.  Published in 1914, by Century Historical
2 CONT Association.
